<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Thornton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1759–1828</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Thornton was a physician, inventor and architect, born to a Quaker family on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jost-van-dyke">Jost Van Dyke</a> in the British Virgin Islands. He won the competition of 1793 for the design of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-capitol">United States Capitol</a> in Washington, and later served as the first superintendent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Patent Office.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-thornton.jpg" alt="William Thornton" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilbert Stuart · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilbert_Stuart,_William_Thornton,_1804,_NGA_1119.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
